{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q010_rossmann_family_function_divergence","answer":"Verified SCOPe facts (release 2.08, the current stable release). All three named groups are distinct families that are siblings under one superfamily. Alcohol dehydrogenase-like, C-terminal domain is family c.2.1.1 (sunid 51736). CoA-binding domain is family c.2.1.8 (sunid 51900). Potassium channel NAD-binding domain is family c.2.1.9 (sunid 63944). Their common parent is superfamily c.2.1, NAD(P)-binding Rossmann-fold domains (sunid 51735), within fold c.2, NAD(P)-binding Rossmann-fold domains (sunid 51734), in class c, alpha and beta proteins (a/b). The fold annotation describes the shared core as three layers a/b/a with a parallel six-stranded beta sheet in order 321456. No homology warnings or heterogeneity flags are recorded on any of the three families or on the superfamily; all four report homology implied at their level. Family c.2.1.1 carries the curator comment that the N-terminal all-beta domain defines the family, meaning the Rossmann domain itself is not what distinguishes it. Family c.2.1.9 is annotated as automatically mapped to Pfam PF02254. Retrieved protein-level children illustrate the functional spread: c.2.1.1 holds zinc-type alcohol dehydrogenases, sorbitol dehydrogenase, quinone oxidoreductase and enoyl reductase domains of fatty acid and polyketide synthases; c.2.1.8 holds the succinyl-CoA synthetase alpha-chain CoA-binding domain, the acetate-CoA ligase alpha chain N-terminal domain and several hypothetical proteins; c.2.1.9 holds the Rck domain of the putative potassium channel Kch, the MthK channel-related protein and the Ktn proteins from B. subtilis and M. jannaschii.

Interpretation. The deepest shared homology-bearing level is the superfamily, so SCOPe asserts probable common ancestry among all three groups: they are treated as descendants of one ancestral dinucleotide-binding Rossmann domain. Because they sit in different families, SCOPe does not claim the closer, usually sequence-detectable relationship that family membership implies. The three families are therefore a textbook case of divergent evolution within a homologous superfamily: one lineage became the catalytic NAD(P)-binding half of two-domain dehydrogenases, one became a CoA-binding module in ligases and synthetases, and one became a cytoplasmic regulatory (RCK/KTN) gating domain of potassium channels that binds nucleotide but is not an enzyme. Superfamily membership supports shared ancestry and a shared nucleotide-binding architecture, not shared biochemical function.

Limits and unsafe transfers. Do not transfer the enzymatic activity, substrate, cofactor specificity or active-site residues of alcohol dehydrogenases to the CoA-binding or potassium-channel domains; SCOPe placement does not establish what ligand any particular member binds, and the question's own names (NAD versus CoA) show the ligand already differs across families. Do not assume the potassium-channel domains are catalytic, or that the CoA-binding domains bind NAD, on the basis of the superfamily label. Do not use an alcohol dehydrogenase structure as an interchangeable homology-modelling template for a channel regulatory domain or a CoA-binding domain without an inspected alignment, because inter-family sequence identity within c.2.1 is typically low and loop, insertion and oligomerisation features differ. The family c.2.1.1 comment shows that its members are defined by a separate N-terminal all-beta domain, so whole-chain comparisons would conflate two domains; compare the Rossmann domains alone. The Pfam mapping on c.2.1.9 is an automated cross-reference, not an independent homology argument, and some proteins in each family are automated matches. The fold-level comment that the nucleotide-binding modes of c.2 and the next two folds are similar is a structural observation; SCOPe records that levels above superfamily do not imply homology, so similarity to other Rossmann-like folds must not be read as a homology claim. Finally, confirming the specific mechanism of functional divergence, or ranking which family is closest to the ancestral state, needs evidence outside SCOPe such as structure-based alignments of the core beta-alpha-beta nucleotide-binding motif, ligand-bound structures, phylogenetic analysis and biochemical assays; none of that was retrieved here.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info","mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__l